export const devotionals42to55: Record<number, { body: string[]; reflection: string[] }> = {
  "42": {
    "body": [
      "Nobody wants to learn to drive from an instructor who has never held a licence. The advice might be accurate, but we would be nervous in the passenger seat. Ezra 7 introduces the book's central human figure with a description that anticipates that worry, and answers it.",
      "The chapter opens with a jump in time. \"After these things, during the reign of Artaxerxes king of Persia, Ezra son of Seraiah, the son of Azariah, the son of Hilkiah...\" (Ezra 7:1). Chapter 6 closed around 515 BC, and Ezra arrives in 458, nearly sixty years later. The events of Esther fall in that gap, which is why we read them first. A whole generation has grown up since the temple was dedicated.",
      "Ezra's family tree runs back \"the son of Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, the son of Aaron the chief priest\" (7:5). The list is shortened, since \"son\" can mean descendant, and Seraiah in verse 1 was the chief priest the Babylonians took prisoner and later executed when Jerusalem fell (2 Kings 25:18–21), well over a century earlier. Ezra's point in giving the line is that he is a priest of Aaron's family, with a legitimate place in Israel's worship.",
      "But the genealogy is not what the passage wants us to remember. \"This Ezra came up from Babylon. He was a teacher well versed in the Law of Moses, which the LORD, the God of Israel, had given\" (7:6). Priests were not always teachers, and the phrase \"well versed\" suggests skill acquired through years of work. Ezra arrives as a scholar as well as a priest.",
      "Then comes the line that gives his journey its stamp: \"The king had granted him everything he asked, for the hand of the LORD his God was on him\" (7:6). That phrase, the hand of the LORD or the gracious hand of God, will recur through the next three chapters (7:9, 7:28, 8:18, 8:22, 8:31). Ezra does not credit his charm, his connections or his scholarship for the king's favour. The hand of God stands behind the royal permission.",
      "The date details are precise. He \"had begun his journey from Babylon on the first day of the first month, and he arrived in Jerusalem on the first day of the fifth month, for the gracious hand of his God was on him\" (7:9). The trip took four months, and Ezra says the same thing again at the end of it.",
      "Verse 10 is the heart of the chapter. \"For Ezra had devoted himself to the study and observance of the Law of the LORD, and to teaching its decrees and laws in Israel.\" The order is deliberate. First he studied the Law. Then he lived it. Only then did he teach it. A teacher who reverses that order is the driving instructor without a licence.",
      "This order protects teaching from two different failures. Study without observance produces a man who knows a great deal and changes nothing, including himself. Observance without study produces enthusiasm that cannot be corrected by the text. Ezra holds all three together, and the chapters ahead show a man shaped by what he studied.",
      "Jesus describes the same pattern in his own words: \"whoever practices and teaches these commands will be called great in the kingdom of heaven\" (Matthew 5:19). Practice comes before teaching there too, and the combination was never meant to be reserved for professionals.",
      "Notice also what Ezra devoted himself to. It was the Law of the LORD, and not a method for reviving Jerusalem. The book that follows is about the effect of a community's being taught by Scripture. Ezra did not arrive merely with a programme. At the heart of his mission was God's word, and he trusted that it would do what it does.",
      "The chapter also quietly corrects a common assumption. It is easy to think that leadership gifts and visible results make a person useful to God. Ezra's introduction puts the weight elsewhere, on years of unglamorous study, a life lived in line with it, and a readiness to pass it on. Everything else in the story grows from that."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Which of the three, studying God's word, living it or teaching it, do you most often skip? What would it look like to take one small step in that direction this week?"
    ]
  },
  "43": {
    "body": [
      "A passport is a small booklet, but it carries the weight of a whole government. When an Australian presents one at a foreign border, the officer is not looking at paper. He is looking at the authority of the country behind it. Ezra travels with something similar, and the details of his document tell us a good deal about how God works through ordinary power.",
      "Ezra 7:12–26 is written in Aramaic, the language of Persian administration, like the letters of chapters 4 to 6. The text introduces it as a copy of the letter Artaxerxes gave Ezra. It opens with the titles: \"Artaxerxes, king of kings, to Ezra the priest, teacher of the Law of the God of heaven\" (7:12). The king calls him by the same description Ezra's own book gave.",
      "The first provision is permission. \"Now I decree that any of the Israelites in my kingdom, including priests and Levites, who volunteer to go to Jerusalem with you, may go\" (7:13). The word \"volunteer\" matters. Artaxerxes does not conscript anyone. Those who go are those who choose to, as in Ezra 1, and as in the freewill offering of Day 14.",
      "Then the king explains Ezra's commission: \"You are sent by the king and his seven advisers to inquire about Judah and Jerusalem with regard to the Law of your God, which is in your hand\" (7:14). The Law is \"in your hand.\" The king has given Ezra a mission to check how closely the community in Jerusalem is living by its own Scripture, and the standard he names is Israel's own book.",
      "Persian kings generally tended to allow conquered peoples to live under their own laws, and Artaxerxes' motives may have included keeping the province stable. Ezra does not tell us the king's reasons. He tells us what he thinks lay behind them: the hand of God (7:6) and the LORD putting it into the king's heart (7:27).",
      "Money follows. \"You are to take with you the silver and gold that the king and his advisers have freely given to the God of Israel, whose dwelling is in Jerusalem\" (7:15). That is a foreign king and his council making a gift to another nation's God. In verse 16 the gifts are expanded: \"together with all the silver and gold you may obtain from the province of Babylon, as well as the freewill offerings of the people and priests for the temple of their God in Jerusalem.\"",
      "Look at how those gifts are layered. The king gives, the surrounding province gives, and the Jewish people give from their own pockets. The temple is being supplied from every side. Yet the offering that most clearly parallels Ezra 1 and Ezra 2 is the freewill one, which came out of the exiles' own hearts and wallets and which no royal order could produce.",
      "The king also gives Ezra liberty with any surplus: \"You and your fellow Israelites may then do whatever seems best with the rest of the silver and gold, in accordance with the will of your God\" (7:18). The phrase in accordance with the will of your God places the decision in Ezra's conscience. Artaxerxes trusts him to know what that is, and a king who does not worship Israel's God defers to Israel's God as the measure.",
      "The passage closes with a generous provision: \"And anything else needed for the temple of your God that you are responsible to supply, you may provide from the royal treasury\" (7:20). We saw something similar in Ezra 6, when Darius paid for the building. The letter shows that God's provision for his work can come from sources that nobody in the community would have chosen.",
      "None of this made Ezra's task easy. He still had to cross the desert, hold the money, and gather the people. But he travelled with a letter in his hand and the Law of God in his other hand. His real authority would turn out to come from the second."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What practical resources, such as time, money or opportunities, has God placed in your hands that you have treated as ordinary? What would it look like to use them as Ezra was told to use his, in accordance with the will of your God?"
    ]
  },
  "44": {
    "body": [
      "Some people believe in doing everything twice. They wear a belt and braces, they back up their back-ups, and they would rather over-insure than be caught short. Artaxerxes seems to be that sort of king. His letter to the treasurers of the province of Trans-Euphrates reads like the work of a man making doubly sure that nothing goes wrong with the God of Israel.",
      "The first part of the decree is generous. \"Now I, King Artaxerxes, decree that all the treasurers of Trans-Euphrates are to provide with diligence whatever Ezra the priest, the teacher of the Law of the God of heaven, may ask of you\" (Ezra 7:21). The treasurers have been ordered to treat Ezra's requests as a royal priority.",
      "Then a list of limits: \"up to a hundred talents of silver, a hundred cors of wheat, a hundred baths of wine, a hundred baths of olive oil, and salt without limit\" (7:22). The cors and baths are ancient measures of grain and liquid, and a talent weighed more than thirty kilograms, so a hundred talents of silver is over three tonnes. The wheat, wine and oil are the supplies for sacrifice, matching what Darius provided in Ezra 6:9. The salt, which was needed for offerings, is \"without limit.\"",
      "The sentence that explains the whole letter comes next. \"Whatever the God of heaven has prescribed, let it be done with diligence for the temple of the God of heaven. Why should his wrath fall on the realm of the king and of his sons?\" (7:23). Artaxerxes is not simply being kind. He is worried. If this God has commanded something, he does not want to be found on the wrong side of it.",
      "That motive is mixed, and we should not tidy it up. Artaxerxes' interest in the temple owes something to fear and something to political prudence. Yet Ezra's book is content to record it, because God does not need the king's motives to be pure to use his resources. We saw the same thing with Darius, who also asked for prayer for himself and his sons (6:10).",
      "The fear itself points to something. Pagan kings with their own gods were willing to take seriously the possibility that the God of Israel might act against them. It echoes the elders' confession to Tattenai, that the exile had come from God's own anger (5:12). Others had noticed that this God was not to be trifled with.",
      "The last part of the decree protects the people who run the temple. \"You are also to know that you have no authority to impose taxes, tribute or duty on any of the priests, Levites, musicians, gatekeepers, temple servants or other workers at this house of God\" (7:24). The list sounds like a staff directory, and it takes in everyone from the priest at the altar to the gatekeeper at the door.",
      "This exemption relieved those who served the temple of one burden, the tax collector. The king, perhaps without realising it, was protecting something the worship of God needed: workers who were free to give their time to it.",
      "Paul draws a related principle for the church when he writes, \"the Lord has commanded that those who proclaim the gospel should receive their living from the gospel\" (1 Corinthians 9:14). That does not mean everyone who serves the church should be paid, or that all ministry must be full-time. Paul himself sometimes worked with his hands (Acts 18:3), and countless volunteers serve faithfully. But a community that wants the gospel proclaimed has a responsibility to think about how faithful service is made possible, whether that is financial support for those who proclaim it or practical help for everyone else who serves.",
      "The decree is a reminder that God's work often depends on unglamorous arrangements. A tax rule, a supply list and a staff directory do not make a stirring sermon text. But the temple could not have functioned without them, and Ezra's book does not pretend otherwise."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Who serves in your church or community in ways that rarely get noticed? What practical support, such as encouragement, time or money, could you give so that they can serve without carrying the whole load?"
    ]
  },
  "45": {
    "body": [
      "Most people thank God after the results come in. The exam is passed, the contract is signed, the scan comes back clear, and then comes the gratitude. Ezra's praise comes before he has packed a single bag. The decree is in his hands, the road has not been walked, and he is already blessing the LORD.",
      "First, though, there is the last section of the king's letter, and it is addressed to Ezra personally. \"And you, Ezra, in accordance with the wisdom of your God, which you possess, appoint magistrates and judges to administer justice to all the people of Trans-Euphrates—all who know the laws of your God. And you are to teach any who do not know them\" (Ezra 7:25).",
      "Two things stand out. The first is that the king assumes Ezra has the wisdom to do this, and calls it the wisdom of his God. The second is the last sentence, which gives Ezra a teaching responsibility. Anyone who does not know the laws of God is to be taught them. The mission of teaching, which Ezra had set himself in verse 10, now has royal backing.",
      "The next verse is severe. \"Whoever does not obey the law of your God and the law of the king must surely be punished by death, banishment, confiscation of property, or imprisonment\" (7:26). The Persian authorities were prepared to enforce the Law with real penalties. It was a serious power, and it will matter later in the book, when the community has to decide how to deal with its own sin.",
      "The scope is wider than Jerusalem. The judges are to serve \"all the people of Trans-Euphrates—all who know the laws of your God,\" which means Jewish communities scattered across that western province. This is not a governorship over half the empire. It is a judicial commission tied to those who know God's Law, together with a responsibility to teach those who do not.",
      "Right here, however, the narrative does something unexpected. Ezra's voice takes over. Until now he has been described in the third person. From verse 27 he writes \"I\" and \"we,\" and the book becomes something close to a personal memoir, which it remains through chapter 9.",
      "The first thing the new voice does is praise. \"Praise be to the LORD, the God of our ancestors, who has put it into the king's heart to bring honor to the house of the LORD in Jerusalem in this way\" (7:27). Ezra's recorded response begins by praising God. He says that the LORD put it into the king's heart, much as Ezra 1:1 described Cyrus, whose heart the LORD moved.",
      "The credit goes where Ezra believes it belongs. A pagan emperor had thought about the matter, made a political decision, and signed a letter. And behind all of it, Ezra sees the hand of the God who moves kings, as we saw on Day 13.",
      "He goes further. God \"has extended his good favor to me before the king and his advisers and all the king's powerful officials\" (7:28). Ezra is aware that he was a minor figure in a very large court. He knows that being heard there was an act of mercy.",
      "The final sentence of the chapter turns outward: \"Because the hand of the LORD my God was on me, I took courage and gathered leaders from Israel to go up with me\" (7:28). Courage is not the starting point. It follows from the recognition that God was at work. Ezra could act because he had first looked at what God had done, and only then at what remained to do.",
      "That is a different order from the one most of us default to. We tend to look at the task, assess our resources, and then decide whether to trust God. Ezra begins with what God has already done and lets that shape the rest. The journey still has four months of danger ahead of it, but he begins by giving thanks."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What has God already done in your situation that you have not yet properly thanked him for? How might naming it change how you approach what is still ahead?"
    ]
  },
  "46": {
    "body": [
      "Many Australian families arrived through what is called chain migration. One relative would go first, find work and a place to live, and then send word back for the others to follow. The second wave often came after the first had already proved it could be done. Ezra 8 describes something like that second wave.",
      "\"These are the family heads and those registered with them who came up with me from Babylon during the reign of King Artaxerxes\" (Ezra 8:1). The chapter then runs through a list of family groups, with a leader named for each. Day 15 covered the great list of Ezra 2. This one is much smaller, and it deserves to be read as a different kind of list.",
      "When we add up the numbers given for the family groups, they come to roughly 1,500 men, with wives, children and others in addition. Compare that with the tens of thousands who returned under Zerubbabel. Ezra's company is a small fraction of the first wave. Many Jews in Babylon had settled lives there and stayed where they were.",
      "The phrase \"registered with them\" is worth noticing. On Day 4 we met priests who could not prove their ancestry and were excluded until a ruling could be made. Ezra's list shows a community that had learned to keep careful records, and each family group has a named head who is answerable for his people on the road. A journey of this size needed that kind of order. Ezra also counts himself among them, \"who came up with me,\" a teacher travelling the same road as the people he will teach.",
      "The names are mostly familiar. Parosh, Pahath-Moab, Zattu, Elam, Shephatiah and others also appeared in Ezra 2. Ezra is not recruiting strangers. He is going back to the same family groups, which fits a picture of relatives of earlier returnees now joining the work their families had begun decades before.",
      "One detail at verse 13 is hard to read with confidence: \"of the descendants of Adonikam, the last ones, whose names were Eliphelet, Jeuel and Shemaiah, and with them 60 men.\" Adonikam's family had already appeared in Ezra 2, so \"the last ones\" most likely marks a later contingent of that family, following relatives who had returned with Zerubbabel. Commentators differ, and we should not build much on one phrase. What the list as a whole suggests is a work passed from one generation to the next.",
      "That is the lesson of the chapter. Cyrus's decree gave the first group its moment, and Artaxerxes' letter and Ezra's invitation gave this group theirs. God's work of restoration did not depend on one dramatic generation. He kept calling new people to the same task, and the book names them.",
      "The list includes priestly families and a descendant of David named Hattush (8:2), a small reminder that the promise to David had not been forgotten, even though there was no king on the throne.",
      "There is also some quiet courage in the list. Four months on the road with children and possessions was not a casual undertaking. The people who chose it were leaving the only home many of them had ever known to settle in a city whose walls were still broken. Ezra 8 does not describe their reasons. It simply records that they went, and the book thought their names worth keeping.",
      "The numbers are small, but numbers have never been the measure in this story. Haggai asked God's people to consider the quality of their obedience, and Zechariah told them it would not be by might or by power. A modest company with the right purpose can do what a larger one with the wrong purpose cannot."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Whose earlier faithfulness are you building on, in your family, your church or your community? What part of that work could you carry forward?"
    ]
  },
  "47": {
    "body": [
      "Before a long trip, a careful traveller does a final check. Ezra's check at the Ahava canal turned up a serious problem. \"I assembled them at the canal that flows toward Ahava, and we camped there three days. When I checked among the people and the priests, I found no Levites there\" (Ezra 8:15).",
      "That was a real gap, and it fits a pattern. Ezra 2:40 had counted only seventy-four Levites among the first returnees, a very small number for a tribe whose work was the temple. Now, sixty years later, none had even presented themselves for the journey.",
      "The Levites assisted in the temple's daily operation, and Ezra's mission involved strengthening the community's worship and obedience to God's Law. Setting out without them would have left the work half-equipped. He could have decided that the priests would be enough. Instead he stopped to fix it.",
      "He sent a delegation of leaders to Iddo, \"the leader in Kasiphia,\" to ask for attendants for the house of God (8:16–17). Then Ezra says, \"Because the gracious hand of our God was on us, they brought us Sherebiah, a capable man\" (8:18). The company that arrived included Sherebiah and his sons and brothers, eighteen in all, and Hashabiah and his relatives, twenty in all, and also \"220 of the temple servants.\" Verse 20 adds, \"All were registered by name.\"",
      "Look at Ezra's explanation for why it worked. It was not because his messengers were persuasive. It was because \"the gracious hand of our God was on us.\" He saw the arrival of the Levites as answered need, not as the result of good management.",
      "Then comes the fast. \"There, by the Ahava Canal, I proclaimed a fast, so that we might humble ourselves before our God and ask him for a safe journey for us and our children, with all our possessions\" (8:21). Unlike Esther, Ezra says plainly what the fast is for. It is a deliberate act of humility, joined with a request for protection.",
      "The reason for the fast follows. \"I was ashamed to ask the king for soldiers and horsemen to protect us from enemies on the road, because we had told the king, 'The gracious hand of our God is on everyone who looks to him, but his great anger is against all who forsake him'\" (8:22). Ezra had told the king that God protects those who seek him. Asking for an armed escort would have looked like going back on his own testimony.",
      "We should read this carefully. Ezra is not laying down a rule that trusting God means refusing practical help. Years later, Nehemiah travelled to Jerusalem with the king's blessing, and recorded that \"the king had also sent army officers and cavalry with me\" (Nehemiah 2:9). He did not treat it as a failure of faith. Ezra's situation was particular: he had made a specific public statement, and consistency mattered for the sake of the king's understanding of God.",
      "The principle underneath is what the psalmist said: \"Some trust in chariots and some in horses, but we trust in the name of the LORD our God\" (Psalm 20:7). The real question in every case is where the heart's confidence sits. Sometimes God provides through an escort, and sometimes without one.",
      "Notice too how the two halves of the passage fit together. In the first half Ezra does the practical work, noticing the missing Levites and sending for them. In the second half he prays. Neither replaces the other. He plans carefully, and he prays dependently. His preparation and his prayers are both expressions of trust in God.",
      "The passage ends with one line: \"So we fasted and petitioned our God about this, and he answered our prayer\" (8:23). Ezra does not describe what the answer looked like yet. That comes in the next section. For now, the point is that he asked, and he was heard."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Do you tend to lean on your own preparation, or to neglect it in the name of faith? What would it look like to do the practical thing and pray with equal seriousness?"
    ]
  },
  "48": {
    "body": [
      "Most churches count the offering with two people in the room. Nobody suspects anyone in particular. It is simply understood that handling other people's gifts is a responsibility, and that honesty is protected by transparency. Ezra 8 describes the same instinct on a much larger scale, carried out in the middle of a desert.",
      "After the fast, Ezra chooses the men who will carry the treasure. He \"set apart twelve\" of the religious leaders, naming Sherebiah and Hashabiah among them, and \"weighed out to them the offering of silver and gold and the articles that the king, his advisers, his officials and all Israel present there had donated for the house of our God\" (Ezra 8:24–25). Translations differ over how to describe these twelve, but the point is clear: trusted men were given responsibility for the treasure. It is weighed in the presence of witnesses before the journey begins.",
      "His charge to them is striking: \"You as well as these articles are consecrated to the LORD. The silver and gold are a freewill offering to the LORD, the God of your ancestors\" (8:28). The people entrusted with the treasure and the treasure itself are described in the same way. Both are set apart for God's use. The men are not only guards; they are part of what is being offered.",
      "Then comes the instruction: \"Guard them carefully until you weigh them out in the chambers of the house of the LORD in Jerusalem before the leading priests and the Levites and the family heads of Israel\" (8:29). Everything will be weighed again at the other end, and the two weighings will be compared. No single person's word will be taken on trust.",
      "Having prayed for protection, Ezra did not skip the safeguards. He saw no conflict between trusting God and being accountable. The fast and the scales belong in the same chapter.",
      "Paul takes the same approach when he describes how the collection for the church in Jerusalem was handled: \"We want to avoid any criticism of the way we administer this liberal gift\" (2 Corinthians 8:20). Good stewardship protects the gift and the reputation of the people handling it.",
      "The journey is covered in a single verse: \"On the twelfth day of the first month we set out from the Ahava Canal to go to Jerusalem. The hand of our God was on us, and he protected us from enemies and bandits along the way\" (8:31). The danger that Ezra feared was real, and he does not pretend it was imaginary. He simply reports that God kept them safe. The prayer of verse 23 had been answered, in the details of the road.",
      "They arrive and rest three days (8:32). On the fourth day the silver, gold and sacred articles are weighed at the temple (8:33), and the result is recorded: \"Everything was accounted for by number and weight, and the entire weight was recorded at that time\" (8:34). Nothing was missing, and the written record proved it.",
      "Next comes worship. \"Then the exiles who had returned from captivity sacrificed burnt offerings to the God of Israel: twelve bulls for all Israel, ninety-six rams, seventy-seven male lambs and, as a sin offering, twelve male goats. All this was a burnt offering to the LORD\" (8:35). The number twelve returns, as it did at the dedication on Day 33. A small company again offers on behalf of the whole people of God, and again a sin offering sits in the middle.",
      "The last verse connects the journey to the province. \"They also delivered the king's orders to the royal satraps and to the governors of Trans-Euphrates, who then gave assistance to the people and to the house of God\" (8:36). Both kinds of officials answered to the king, and their cooperation mattered. The royal letter of chapter 7 does its work. The officials respond, as Tattenai did earlier, and the community receives the support it was promised.",
      "That closes the account of Ezra's journey. Ezra has arrived, the treasure is safe, the worship is strengthened, and the officials are cooperative. Everything seems to be going well. The next chapter opens with a report that changes the mood completely."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Where in your own life do transparency and trust belong together? Is there an area of responsibility, such as money, time or influence, where you would benefit from being accountable to someone else?"
    ]
  },
  "49": {
    "body": [
      "Termites rarely announce themselves. A house can look sound for years, with fresh paint and solid doors, while something quiet works through the timber out of sight. The owner who finally taps a wall and hears the hollow sound understands very quickly how long the damage has been going on. Ezra 9 opens with a tap on the wall.",
      "The journey from Babylon has ended well. The treasure has been weighed, the offerings made and the governors have cooperated. Then, \"After these things had been done, the leaders came to me and said, 'The people of Israel, including the priests and the Levites, have not kept themselves separate from the neighboring peoples with their detestable practices'\" (Ezra 9:1). A list of nations follows, and then the specific charge: \"They have taken some of their daughters as wives for themselves and their sons, and have mingled the holy race with the peoples around them. And the leaders and officials have led the way in this unfaithfulness\" (9:2).",
      "D. A. Carson notes that readers shaped by individualism and relativism struggle with Ezra's reaction. Just over a hundred men are named at the end of the book, in a community of tens of thousands, and Ezra reacts as if it were a catastrophe.",
      "First, we should deal with the phrase \"holy race.\" In English it can sound ethnic, as if the problem were blood. But look at what verse 1 actually complains about: \"their detestable practices.\" The concern is religious. Deuteronomy gave the reason long before: foreign spouses \"will turn your children away from following me to serve other gods\" (Deuteronomy 7:4).",
      "The concern was not racial purity in the modern sense. Israel's covenant identity involved belonging to a particular people, but its deepest concern was allegiance to the LORD. Matthew's genealogy of the Messiah includes \"Rahab\" and \"Ruth\" (Matthew 1:5), a Canaanite woman and a Moabite woman who turned to Israel's God, and on Day 34 we saw that outsiders who bound themselves to the LORD found a place among his people. We should also be careful not to assume that every foreign wife was personally an idolater. Ezra reports a serious pattern of covenant unfaithfulness without describing each marriage. What he fears is Israel being drawn toward other gods.",
      "Now look at how Ezra responds. \"When I heard this, I tore my tunic and cloak, pulled hair from my head and beard and sat down appalled\" (9:3). He does not call a meeting or write a denunciation. He does not even speak. He tears his clothes and tears at his own hair and beard, and then he sits.",
      "That is the reaction of a man who loves the people. A condemnation would have been easier, and it would have cost him nothing. Grief costs a great deal, and it identifies him with those he is grieving over.",
      "The next verse shows that he is not alone. \"Then everyone who trembled at the words of the God of Israel gathered around me because of this unfaithfulness of the exiles\" (9:4). Some in the community still took God's word seriously enough to be shaken by it. Isaiah had described the people God favours as those \"humble and contrite in spirit, and who tremble at my word\" (Isaiah 66:2). A small group of people like that had survived, and they came to sit with Ezra.",
      "\"And I sat there appalled until the evening sacrifice.\" He sits for hours. The prayer that will follow in the next verses does not come from a quick emotional reaction, but from a long and silent absorbing of what has happened. We should notice how unusual that is in a culture that expects an immediate public statement on everything.",
      "Carson draws out one more thing that makes the chapter relevant to us. Sin is not only individual but corporate, and it tends to enter a community by the back door. Verse 2 says that the leaders and officials led the way. When those in authority treat a compromise as ordinary, everyone else learns that it is. Few people in the community would have planned to turn from God. They simply stopped noticing the hollow sound in the wall."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Is there something you have grown used to that God's word describes in far stronger terms than you do? Who around you trembles at his word, and could you sit with them?"
    ]
  },
  "50": {
    "body": [
      "At the time of the evening sacrifice, Ezra gets up. \"Then, at the evening sacrifice, I rose from my self-abasement, with my tunic and cloak torn, and fell on my knees with my hands spread out to the LORD my God\" (Ezra 9:5). The hours of silence have ended. What follows is one of the great prayers of the Bible, and it begins not with a request but with shame.",
      "\"I am too ashamed and disgraced, my God, to lift up my face to you, because our sins are higher than our heads and our guilt has reached to the heavens\" (9:6). Ezra says \"my God,\" so the relationship is intact. But he cannot meet God's eyes. The words are a picture of a man who knows exactly how serious the matter is.",
      "Notice the pronouns. Ezra says \"our sins\" and \"our guilt,\" though the text gives no hint that he had married a foreign wife himself. He stands with the people and takes their guilt as his own. Carson points out that Isaiah did the same, when he cried out in the temple that he was \"a man of unclean lips\" and that he lived \"among a people of unclean lips\" (Isaiah 6:5). Daniel's prayer, which we read on Day 12, speaks the same way: \"we have sinned.\"",
      "A community that can only say \"they\" when sin is mentioned will never repent together. Ezra's \"we\" is a refusal to stand outside the people he loves. It is also a quiet echo of someone greater, because the New Testament says that God \"made him who had no sin to be sin for us, so that in him we might become the righteousness of God\" (2 Corinthians 5:21). Ezra identifies with his people's corporate guilt, as a member of the community standing before God. Christ does something different in kind: as the sinless one, he uniquely bears the sins of others.",
      "Then Ezra reads the present through the past. \"From the days of our ancestors until now, our guilt has been great. Because of our sins, we and our kings and our priests have been subjected to the sword and captivity, to pillage and humiliation at the hand of foreign kings, as it is today\" (9:7). Carson highlights this as the first of Ezra's insights: the exile was caused by sin, and some of that sin came through close ties with the surrounding peoples. Ezra has learned the lesson of the first half of this devotional. He knows why Jerusalem fell, and he fears the same pattern is starting again.",
      "Verse 8 turns toward grace, but with great restraint. \"But now, for a brief moment, the LORD our God has been gracious in leaving us a remnant and giving us a firm place in his sanctuary, and so our God gives light to our eyes and a little relief in our bondage.\" Everything in that sentence is small. A brief moment, a remnant, a firm place, a little relief. Ezra does not inflate what God has done. He sees it as real mercy, and he sees it as fragile.",
      "This is the second insight Carson draws out. The return is a brief moment of grace and not the end of the story. We saw the same tension in Psalm 126, where God's people remembered a restoration and still prayed for another. Ezra is living between the two, and it makes the report of verse 2 more serious, because he is looking at the loss of something precious and recent.",
      "He keeps going: \"Though we are slaves, our God has not forsaken us in our bondage. He has shown us kindness in the sight of the kings of Persia: He has granted us new life to rebuild the house of our God and repair its ruins, and he has given us a wall of protection in Judah and Jerusalem\" (9:9). The word \"slaves\" is honest, for they remain subjects of a foreign empire. Yet Ezra names the kindness that has come through Persian kings.",
      "The \"wall of protection\" is probably a figure of speech for God's protection, since the actual walls of Jerusalem were still broken when Nehemiah arrived. Ezra uses the image anyway, and it points ahead to the book that follows."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "When you pray about sin, do you say \"they\" or \"we\"? Is there a community you belong to, such as a family, a church or a nation, whose failings you have been willing to describe but not to grieve over?"
    ]
  },
  "51": {
    "body": [
      "Most prayers about sin have a turning point. The person confesses, and then asks for relief. \"Forgive me,\" we say, \"and help me not to do it again.\" Ezra's prayer reaches its turning point and does something unexpected. It does not turn. It simply ends, with God's righteousness and Israel's guilt side by side.",
      "The last section begins with a question that has no answer. \"But now, our God, what can we say after this? For we have forsaken the commands you gave through your servants the prophets when you said: 'The land you are entering to possess is a land polluted by the corruption of its peoples. By their detestable practices they have filled it with their impurity from one end to the other. Therefore, do not give your daughters in marriage to their sons or take their daughters for your sons'\" (Ezra 9:10–12).",
      "Ezra summarises God's earlier commands, which he credits to \"your servants the prophets\" and which draw on the Law's warnings about the land. God had been clear. The command was not hidden or obscure. The people had known it, and had set it aside.",
      "This is the third thing Carson sees in the prayer. Given the clear command, and given everything God had done for Israel in the exodus and the exile, the marriages are ingratitude and defiance. They are the choice of people who have received a rescue and have decided to treat it lightly. It is difficult to imagine that scale of generosity being met with indifference, and yet that is what Ezra believes has happened.",
      "Then the verse that reverses expectations: \"What has happened to us is a result of our evil deeds and our great guilt, and yet, our God, you have punished us less than our sins deserved and have given us a remnant like this\" (9:13). Carson names this as the fifth insight, that God's people have sinned far worse than the punishment they have received. The exile was a severe judgment. Ezra says that it was still less than they deserved.",
      "Not every hardship we experience is God's punishment for a particular sin. Ezra is speaking about the exile, whose cause the prophets had already explained. But his reading of it is worth noticing. Ezra sees the remnant itself as an act of mercy, and it changes how he reads everything else. He is not asking why God has been hard. He is amazed that God has been so patient.",
      "The prayer then asks a question that would not look out of place in the middle of a courtroom. \"Shall we then break your commands again and intermarry with the peoples who commit such detestable practices? Would you not be angry enough with us to destroy us, leaving us no remnant or survivor?\" (9:14). There is no attempt to defend the people. Ezra assumes that if the pattern continues, God would be right to end it.",
      "It closes with two sentences. \"LORD, the God of Israel, you are righteous! We are left this day as a remnant. Here we are before you in our guilt, though because of it not one of us can stand in your presence\" (9:15). The prayer is addressed to a righteous God, by people who cannot stand before him. Nothing is left to add.",
      "Look closely at what is missing. From verse 6 to verse 15, Ezra does not ask God for anything. There is no petition for forgiveness, no request for a way forward and no bargaining. He simply places himself and the people in front of the one who is righteous and leaves it there. Confession of this kind is a form of trust: he believes God will deal justly and he does not try to manage the result.",
      "The New Testament gives the answer that Ezra's prayer leaves open. \"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just and will forgive us our sins and purify us from all unrighteousness\" (1 John 1:9). The God who is righteous has also made a way for sinners to stand. Ezra did not yet know how, but his prayer is shaped to receive it."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "When you confess, how quickly do you move to asking for relief? What would it be like to stay for a moment with the plain fact that God is righteous, and let that be enough?"
    ]
  },
  "52": {
    "body": [
      "Ezra did not plan a campaign. He did not organise a petition, call a meeting or announce an agenda. He prayed. \"While Ezra was praying and confessing, weeping and throwing himself down before the house of God, a large crowd of Israelites—men, women and children—gathered around him. They too wept bitterly\" (Ezra 10:1). A gathering that began around Ezra in chapter 9 has now grown into a crowd.",
      "The scale of the response matters. In chapter 9 only \"everyone who trembled\" had joined him. Now a large crowd of men, women and children weeps with him. Public grief of this kind cannot be arranged. It happens when people see something they had missed and are moved by it.",
      "Then one man speaks. \"Then Shekaniah son of Jehiel, one of the descendants of Elam, said to Ezra, 'We have been unfaithful to our God by marrying foreign women from the peoples around us. But in spite of this, there is still hope for Israel'\" (10:2). It is worth noticing who Shekaniah is. Descendants of Elam appear later on the list of men who had married foreign women (10:26), and a Jehiel from Elam, possibly his father, is among them. He is not an outsider pointing a finger. He is speaking from inside the problem.",
      "His sentence has two halves, and both matter. First comes a plain confession: \"We have been unfaithful.\" He says \"we,\" as Ezra had. Then comes hope: \"But in spite of this, there is still hope for Israel.\" The hope does not deny the unfaithfulness. It grows out of facing it. A community that cannot name what has gone wrong has nothing to repair.",
      "The proposal that follows is severe. \"Now let us make a covenant before our God to send away all these women and their children, in accordance with the counsel of my lord and of those who fear the commands of our God. Let it be done according to the Law\" (10:3). The plan is to separate from the foreign wives and from their children.",
      "We should hold back from rushing to a verdict. This chapter is hard for readers who care both about faithfulness and about families, and the text itself does not make the question easy. D. A. Carson sets out two ways in which Christians have read what happens next. One sees a courageous revival, in which people pay a heavy price to put God first. The other sees a right prayer followed by a wrong action. We will consider both as the chapter unfolds. For now, notice the shape of what is happening.",
      "The proposal comes from the people, and not from Ezra. Shekaniah also appeals to those who \"fear the commands of our God,\" and to the counsel of \"my lord,\" presumably Ezra. Then he hands responsibility back: \"Rise up; this matter is in your hands. We will support you, so take courage and do it\" (10:4). The speaker is both confessing and asking Ezra to lead.",
      "Ezra's response is measured. \"So Ezra rose up and put the leading priests and Levites and all Israel under oath to do what had been suggested. And they took the oath\" (10:5). The leading priests and Levites are explicitly included in the oath, fittingly so, since the leaders had helped set the pattern of unfaithfulness. Those who had set the pattern must also be bound to the new one.",
      "Then Ezra withdraws. \"Then Ezra withdrew from before the house of God and went to the room of Jehohanan son of Eliashib. While he was there, he ate no food and drank no water, because he continued to mourn over the unfaithfulness of the exiles\" (10:6). The oath has been taken, but he does not celebrate. He goes into seclusion to keep mourning.",
      "That detail deserves attention. Whatever one thinks of the plan, Ezra does not treat it as a triumph.  Ezra is fasting. His grief outlasts the decision."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "When you are faced with a failure, in yourself or in your community, do you look first for a plan or for honest grief? What might it mean to let hope grow out of an honest \"we have been unfaithful\"?"
    ]
  },
  "53": {
    "body": [
      "The ninth month falls in what we would call November and December, the wet season in Judah. That explains the scene in Ezra 10:9: all the men of Judah and Benjamin sitting in the open square before the house of God, \"greatly distressed by the occasion and because of the rain.\"",
      "They have been summoned. \"A proclamation was then issued throughout Judah and Jerusalem for all the exiles to assemble in Jerusalem\" (Ezra 10:7). The penalty for staying away was severe: \"Anyone who failed to appear within three days would forfeit all his property, in accordance with the decision of the officials and elders, and would himself be expelled from the assembly of the exiles\" (10:8).",
      "Ezra's address is brief. \"You have been unfaithful; you have married foreign women, adding to Israel's guilt. Now honor the LORD, the God of your ancestors, and do his will. Separate yourselves from the peoples around you and from your foreign wives\" (10:10–11). The crowd answers with one voice: \"You are right! We must do as you say\" (10:12).",
      "But then they raise a practical difficulty. \"But there are many people here and it is the rainy season; so we cannot stand outside. Besides, this matter cannot be taken care of in a day or two, because we have sinned greatly in this thing\" (10:13). They propose a process: officials to act for the assembly, and then each man with a foreign wife to appear at a set time with the elders and judges of his town (10:14).",
      "Here the text slows down. The assembly does not act in a rush of emotion. It demands time, structure and local judges. The result: \"On the first day of the tenth month they sat down to investigate the cases, and by the first day of the first month they finished dealing with all the men who had married foreign women\" (10:16–17). It took about three months.",
      "Men were chosen \"from each family division, and all of them designated by name\" (10:16). The process was orderly, but an orderly process is not the same as a just one, and the text tells us little about how the wives and children were treated.",
      "One small verse breaks the unanimity: \"Only Jonathan son of Asahel and Jahzeiah son of Tikvah, supported by Meshullam and Shabbethai the Levite, opposed this\" (10:15). The NIV reads these four men as opponents of the plan, although translations differ on the details. The book does not tell us their reasons, but it preserves their names and lets the dissent stand.",
      "D. A. Carson describes two readings of this chapter. On the first, this is revival. The people take God's word seriously enough to pay a heavy price, loving God more than their families. The lesson is that sin must be dealt with radically.",
      "On the second reading, the prayer of chapter 9 was right, but the action of chapter 10 was not. Genesis 2:24 describes the foundational union of marriage: \"a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and they become one flesh.\" The Law regulated divorce but did not simply commend it. And sending children away would have put them at risk of losing their place in the covenant community, though the surviving account does not tell us what became of them. Carson notes that less drastic measures were available, such as forbidding future marriages of this kind, removing intermarried priests from office, and leading the community in a fresh study of the Law.",
      "Ezra 10 does not announce a word from the LORD commanding the separations. It reports a decision made by the community \"according to the Law\" (10:3). That does not prove the decision was wrong, but it does mean we should be careful before treating it as a model.",
      "Anyone reading this by itself should be clear that Ezra 10 cannot simply be used to tell a Christian to divorce an unbelieving spouse. Paul says the opposite about existing marriages: \"If any brother has a wife who is not a believer and she is willing to live with him, he must not divorce her\" (1 Corinthians 7:12), and he says the same of a wife with an unbelieving husband (7:13). Malachi will speak to this area of life on Day 99. For now, read the passage slowly and with humility. The costs were real, and they fell on people who had no voice in the assembly."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What does it take, in your own life, to make a costly change without acting in haste or harshness? Who can help you weigh a hard decision carefully?"
    ]
  },
  "54": {
    "body": [
      "Most histories leave out the embarrassing names. A writer who wants a story to end well will usually avoid listing the people who failed. Ezra 10 does the opposite. It closes with a roll call of more than a hundred men, each one named, each one guilty of the same offence, and it begins with the priests. \"Among the descendants of the priests, the following had married foreign women\" (Ezra 10:18).",
      "The first family on the list belongs to the high priest. \"From the descendants of Joshua son of Jozadak, and his brothers: Maaseiah, Eliezer, Jarib and Gedaliah\" (10:18). That is the Joshua of Haggai and Zechariah, the man whose filthy garments were replaced in Zechariah's vision (Day 26). The book does not hide that his own relatives were among those who needed to repent.",
      "Then the priests' response: \"They all gave their hands in pledge to put away their wives, and for their guilt they each presented a ram from the flock as a guilt offering\" (10:19). They did not only promise. They also brought an offering for their guilt, which acknowledges that this was sin and not just a policy. The list of Levites, singers, gatekeepers and other Israelites continues through verse 43, with more than a hundred names in all.",
      "The final verse ends the book of Ezra on an unsettled note. \"All these had married foreign women, and some of them had children by these wives\" (10:44). There is no concluding verse about renewed peace or restored joy. The text simply stops. It does not tell us what happened to the women or the children, and we should not invent it.",
      "So where does that leave us? D. A. Carson's verdict is worth following, as an assessment of a difficult narrative. The text itself does not settle whether this was revival or error, and Scripture does not positively judge Ezra's decision to be a mistake. Carson suspects that both readings are partly right, and compares it to Gideon, Jephthah and Samson, whose stories also mix real faith with serious failure. Here too something noble and something heartless may sit together. Some sins are so tangled that even a repentant people's solutions come out messy.",
      "That mixed verdict protects us from two mistakes. One is to treat Ezra 10 as a command for Christians, as though the right response to a compromised church were a mass separation of families. The other is to dismiss the whole chapter as fanaticism and miss the seriousness of the sin it responds to.",
      "The New Testament holds the question with more care. \"Do not be yoked together with unbelievers\" (2 Corinthians 6:14) warns about close partnership with those who do not share faith in Christ. But where a marriage already exists, the instruction is different: \"If any brother has a wife who is not a believer and she is willing to live with him, he must not divorce her\" (1 Corinthians 7:12). And Jesus, asked about divorce, pointed back to creation: \"what God has joined together, let no one separate\" (Matthew 19:6). Whatever else Ezra 10 teaches, it is not a licence to dissolve marriages.",
      "What it does teach is the seriousness of unfaithfulness, and how easily compromise can become normal unless God's people stay attentive to his word. Ezra's generation acted, and the effort cost them.",
      "Yet look at how the book of Ezra ends. After the prayer, the oath, the assembly and the three months of investigation, the problem is not described as solved. We will meet it again, because in Nehemiah 13 the same issue returns a generation later. Outward reform, even sincere and costly reform, cannot reach the heart. That is why the promises of Jeremiah and Ezekiel in the first half of this devotional matter so much. God had promised a new heart, and nothing less would ever be enough."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "Where in your own life have you tried to deal with a deep problem by changing outward behaviour alone? What would it mean to bring the problem to God and ask him for the change only he can make?"
    ]
  },
  "55": {
    "body": [
      "Ezra's story closes around 457 BC. Nehemiah does not appear until about a dozen years later. Between the two books lies a quiet stretch in which we hear little about what happened in Jerusalem. The people have confessed, taken oaths and cleared up what they could, and the next stage of the story has not begun. Waiting is part of the story as well.",
      "Psalm 130 is another of the Songs of Ascents, and it suits a gap like that. It begins in a place no one chooses to go. \"Out of the depths I cry to you, LORD; Lord, hear my voice. Let your ears be attentive to my cry for mercy\" (Psalm 130:1–2). The depths are a picture of trouble so deep that the person can only call upward. The psalm does not say what caused it, which leaves room for many kinds of trouble.",
      "Then the psalmist turns inward and sees the root. \"If you, LORD, kept a record of sins, Lord, who could stand?\" (130:3). That question echoes Ezra's final words. In Ezra 9:15 he said, \"not one of us can stand in your presence.\" The psalmist agrees. Nobody could stand if God kept an account.",
      "But the next verse contains the turn that the whole psalm depends on. \"But with you there is forgiveness, so that we can, with reverence, serve you\" (130:4). The forgiveness is not a reason to take sin lightly. It is the reason the psalmist can serve God with reverence in the first place. A God who forgave nothing would produce fear and hiding, and not worship.",
      "Then the psalm settles into waiting. \"I wait for the LORD, my whole being waits, and in his word I put my hope\" (130:5). The waiting is not passive. It is anchored to the word of God, which means to promises already spoken. Israel had a great deal of that: the covenant, the prophets, the promise of a new heart, the Branch. Waiting meant holding on to what had been said.",
      "Verse 6 gives us one of the most vivid images in the Psalms. \"I wait for the LORD more than watchmen wait for the morning, more than watchmen wait for the morning.\" The repetition is deliberate. Anyone who has stood a night watch knows how slowly the hours go, and also how certain the dawn is. The watchman does not wonder whether morning will come. He is only waiting for it to arrive.",
      "The psalm then widens from one person to the whole nation. \"Israel, put your hope in the LORD, for with the LORD is unfailing love and with him is full redemption\" (130:7). The psalmist is no longer speaking only for himself. He is calling the community to wait together. And the final verse names what they are waiting for: \"He himself will redeem Israel from all their sins\" (130:8).",
      "That last line deserves a moment. Not \"from their enemies\" or \"from exile,\" but \"from all their sins.\" After the painful reforms of Ezra 9 and 10, in which a community struggled to confront its sin, the psalm points to a deeper redemption that God himself will provide. The reforms of Ezra could address what people did. Only God could address what they were.",
      "This is the thread the New Testament picks up. The angel told Joseph that Mary's child was to be named Jesus, \"because he will save his people from their sins\" (Matthew 1:21). The psalm's last line and the angel's announcement are two ends of the same promise.",
      "The silence between Ezra and Nehemiah can be read in that light. Nothing seems to be happening. Yet the psalm tells us that God's people were never waiting for nothing. They were waiting for the one who would redeem Israel from all their sins, and the waiting, for all its slowness, was already moving toward the morning."
    ],
    "reflection": [
      "What are you waiting for that has gone quiet? What word of God could you hold onto, as the psalmist did, while the night watch continues?"
    ]
  }
};
